# Binary test fixtures — prevent CRLF normalization on commit/checkout.
# Captured terminal scrollback contains raw ESC sequences and mixed
# line endings that must round-trip byte-exact through Git on both
# POSIX and Windows checkouts.
server/src/terminal/fixtures/*.log binary

# Record-boundary parity fixture (iterate-2026-07-18-triage-jsonl-record-
# boundary). DELIBERATELY mixes LF and CRLF line endings and carries
# intra-line corruption; the committed expected-output JSON is generated from
# these exact bytes by the real Python reader. EOL normalization erases the
# CRLF case the cross-language parity gate asserts — verified: without this
# rule `core.autocrlf=true` stored 0 CR bytes for a file with 1.
server/src/test/fixtures/triage-recovery.jsonl binary

# Shipwright append-log union merge driver (managed block — do not hand-edit).
shipwright_events.jsonl merge=union
.shipwright/triage.jsonl merge=union

.shipwright/agent_docs/architecture.md merge=union
.shipwright/agent_docs/conventions.md merge=union

# Shell scripts must stay LF regardless of a contributor's core.autocrlf.
# They are authored on Windows but run on macOS/Linux (the production
# rebuild+restart helpers, the hook installer). A CRLF `.sh` fails there
# with "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M", so pin the line endings.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same rule, same reason, for the ONE shebang file this repo publishes as an
# executable: the npm `bin` entry of @svenroth-ai/shipwright. `npx` runs it
# through its shebang, so a CRLF checkout packs `/usr/bin/env node^M` and the
# installer dies on macOS/Linux with "bad interpreter". It also breaks the
# Windows dev loop today: esbuild's hashbang handling leaves the stray CR, so
# `bootstrapper/test/bin.test.mjs` fails to parse with "SyntaxError: Invalid or
# unexpected token" in any autocrlf worktree — green on Linux CI, red locally.
# The sibling scripts/*.mjs are spawned as `node <path>` (shebang decorative),
# so they need no pin. Verified with `git ls-files --eol`.
bootstrapper/bin/shipwright.mjs text eol=lf

# iterate-2026-07-29-accepted-risk-ci-gate — the accepted-risk drift guard hashes
# these files byte-for-byte (`scripts/ci/accepted_risks_vendor.json`), and this
# repo runs core.autocrlf=true. Without this pin the working tree gets CRLF on a
# Windows checkout while the manifest records the LF hash, so the guard fails
# with "{name} has been edited in place" on a file nobody touched — and the
# remedy that message prescribes (re-record the hashes) would then turn Linux CI
# permanently red. Verified with `git ls-files --eol` (NOT `git show :path`,
# which smudges under autocrlf). Also keeps the vendored code from being
# executed with CRLF.
# Recursive since iterate-2026-07-31-revendor-accepted-risk-gate. The old globs
# were `scripts/ci/*.py` + `scripts/ci/tests/*.py`, i.e. exactly today's two
# levels, so a module vendored one directory deeper would have had NO pin — and
# the reverse-drift guard was non-recursive in the same way, so it would have been
# invisible to both. That combination produces precisely the misleading failure
# this pin exists to prevent: a Windows checkout yields CRLF, the manifest records
# the LF hash, and the guard reports "edited in place" on a file nobody touched.
scripts/ci/**/*.py text eol=lf
scripts/ci/*.py text eol=lf
scripts/ci/accepted_risks_vendor.json text eol=lf
